      "knowledge_point_id": "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form",
      "problem": "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line.",
      "worked_solution": "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(1,-2,3)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨4,3,-1⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{4}=\\frac{y-(-2)}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{4}=\\frac{y+2}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}.\n$$",
