    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-32829",
      "knowledge_point_id": "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/58076d96d149ff4521103fd15c7187bc1a58595ab20f28be92be0f79385aee61.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above.",
      "worked_solution": "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,1,4)$ and $Q(3,-2,2).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨3,-2,2⟩-⟨-1,1,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-1)}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{-2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{-2}.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
