      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line",
      "problem": "Find a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/792bf09ff2546903b863a879a2518c42bbd30fdfce652bd4e22b1ad19aefc7c9.png)",
      "worked_solution": "We know that the line passes through the points ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/8f40930bbc65b25e23296d4a6fae2bb12da4713b1b65a0444ed6aedb8287f857.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/10c3f2aff07e3e42290e5fddc4751c493a0ca3839907c32a627bcbdbcc6c8aa8.png) Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/6c751380dede71816d826d31daf38dbcd4f34b6b462de00ed5071bd366a6645c.png) which is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/e3ae667c43eafd8804a9fb7033c9040c5d4cca11b0c90368f7dc5a7cfda35934.png)\n\nUsing the point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/c9b75fa5f325825788ae2a64e227779c81af2454230ded498d7245dd9e0c66cf.png) and the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/dee4f9a47a1bdff7b5bee4b328768e8967ff52dba70800b6defa0575a7034847.png), we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/8f28616529da6a43199b0f9bfe74c186544f84c111f58ae43d066c2089acc921.png)\nor equivalently\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/158ba3699f056c1d67ae3c56699c266a4cc99c6e0d5d13f70dae21c04a5f54bf.png)\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/9d8d4e18a0bed3777184a8299ba2f3183c3a43740f75103f18e2e910f27f8b40.png) involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/d0a37c7a07a4a5e017e3edb659fad8defff4fa33a3accbd0e6fd2d6d8439cf94.png) component always maintains the constant value ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/b7f9100157159abea8f5f7a86a272d5a539e2d8084e8c894642bc49342e2a76f.png) For example, our two points ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/ad5d9528b6a85fa9d0e010a6c7b0fe7d51253b71fe995a2010204708256aa081.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/5a762bf2a30c7fb3be3ba2e1d8abf1b308614d123e11600d93d7b3b02fe828c7.png) both have ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/fdc688a7d7010425aace3db801008b0b83b950e7ff7c03a89f9745d4651e1dca.png)-coordinates of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/e80fbe48c17ecbcad07d73a5822a6bc6c86aa7e20cbc7df457daebbcc98d3ccb.png)\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/634bc23451d22fdadb3de9c4d9fc8c4559e1b79cefaf5771b541373a33f3ef9a.png) part and instead write ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/c8e5605672eaf10428132773817bccd6795c20281686d5bcb272370619b4b81d.png) as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/6f956b080575cea02674247877e555d3158f184f55bff45147011be97c4b474b.png)",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
